          "type": "user",
          "text": "# AI SDK Chatbot Message Persistence Implementation\n\n## Key Interfaces & Types\n\nThe AI SDK provides `UIMessage[]` as the standard format for storing chat messages. This format includes additional metadata like `id` and `createdAt` that are essential for persistence, distinguishing it from the `ModelMessage` format used internally.\n\n## Custom Storage Implementation\n\nThe documentation emphasizes that \"the function interfaces are designed to be easily replaced with other implementations.\" The guide demonstrates a file-based approach using:\n\n- `createChat()`: Generates unique chat IDs and initializes storage\n- `loadChat(id)`: Retrieves persisted messages from storage\n- `saveChat({chatId, messages})`: Persists complete message arrays\n\nDevelopers can substitute file operations with database calls or cloud storage services without changing the interface contract.\n\n## Validation Requirements\n\nWhen loading messages containing tools, metadata, or custom data parts, use `validateUIMessages()` before processing. This ensures messages match current schema definitions and handles validation errors gracefully, which is critical when message structures evolve over time.\n\n## Storage Best Practices\n\n**Server-side ID generation** is recommended for persistence to ensure consistency across sessions. Two approaches exist:\n\n1. Using `generateMessageId` with `createIdGenerator()` in `toUIMessageStreamResponse`\n2. Controlling message IDs via `createUIMessageStream` with custom start message parts\n\n**Client disconnect handling** requires calling `result.consumeStream()` to ensure backend storage completes even after network disconnection, preventing incomplete conversations from being lost.\n\nNo built-in adapters are provided—the SDK deliberately delegates storage implementation to developers for maximum flexibility.",
